    "summary": "High‑energy set with smoking Help>Slip>Killer and tight jams",
    "blurb": "The Oct 12 1983 show bursts with nonstop energy, highlighted by a scorching Help On The Way → Slipknot! → Franklin's Tower suite and a punchy rhythm section. Jerry’s guitar shreds while Phil steps up on vocals for Cold Rain And Snow. Standout moments include Cumberland Blues, Ramble On Rose, Black Peter and a rousing Revolution encore.",
    "review": "On October 12, 1983 at Madison Square Garden the Dead were in peak form. The first set opened with a crisp rendition of Cold Rain And Snow that featured Phil Lesh’s surprising vocal turn and Jerry Garcia’s crystal‑clear lead, then rolled through a tight sequence of New Minglewood Blues, Ramble On Rose and a memorable Cumberland Blues. Highlights kept building as the band launched into the legendary Help On The Way → Slipknot! → Franklin's Tower trio – described across recordings as “smoking” and “super‑charged.”\n\nThe second set maintained that momentum with Man Smart, Woman Smarter, a tight He’s Gone into the classic drum‑and‑space excursion, then a seamless flow through Truckin’, Black Peter and Not Fade Away. The encore closed with an inspired Revolution, cementing the night as one of the tour’s most exhilarating outings. Jerry’s “filthy” solo work, Brent’s subtle keyboard textures, and the twin‑drum engine of Mickey Hart and Bill Kreutzmann kept the groove driving forward throughout.",
